FINAL ANSWER:
Ask **Troy Goode** first. Douglas Christopher Wilson and Alejandro Caravaca Puchades are the next choices. I ranked people by how many of their lines are still in `lib/index.js` today (`surviving-lines --reviewers`, `git blame -w -M -C`). The file has 214 non-blank lines (238 with blank lines).

| # | Reviewer | Surviving lines | Share | Commits | Last commit | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Troy Goode (2 addresses) | 144 | 67.3% | 29 | 2026-04-10 | active |
| 2 | Douglas Christopher Wilson | 20 | 9.3% | 4 | 2017-10-04 | inactive |
| 3 | Alejandro Caravaca Puchades | 14 | 6.5% | 5 | 2015-05-28 | inactive |

LuisEGR and Raymond Feng have 6 lines each (2.8%). 19 more authors hold the rest.

**Two addresses for one person (a guess):** I count Troy Goode as one person. The name is the same and git has no record that the two addresses belong to one person, so this is my guess.
- <email redacted>: 132 lines (61.7%), 28 commits, last commit 2016-08-23 (inactive)
- troy@laptop.example: 12 lines (5.6%), 1 commit, 2026-04-10 (active)

If you confirm it is one person, this `.mailmap` line makes the merge permanent. I did not write it:
`Troy Goode <<email redacted>> Troy Goode <troy@laptop.example>`

Alejandro's 14 lines already include the commits under the name `alex94puchades`, which uses the same address.

**Active or not:** "Active" means at least one commit in the year before 2026-04-12. Only Troy Goode is active, and only through the laptop address. Wilson and Caravaca Puchades have not committed in years. They are still the next-largest owners, so they are worth asking, but they may not reply.

**How concentrated the file is:** The bus factor is the smallest number of authors whose surviving lines together reach 50% of the file. For this file it is 1, Troy Goode alone (67.3%). With the two addresses kept separate it is still 1 (61.7%). To reach 80% takes 3 authors merged, or 4 unmerged.

**What I left out of the counts:**
- Commit `62628c0` is not counted. It is the "style reformat" commit listed in `.git-blame-ignore-revs`.
- Commit `fcd2b56` ("switch to ESLint; clean up", Troy Goode, 6 surviving lines) looked mechanical to the tool. It is a real cleanup with deletions, so I kept it. Leaving it out does not change any of the figures.
- The figures show who wrote the code that is still there. They do not show who knows the code best or who did reviews in the past.

